  "payload": {
    "rawResponseText": "An owner who wants to **preserve individuality** should usually look first at **soft brands** and, in some cases, **collection/consortia brands**. Soft brands let the hotel keep its own name, design, and character while gaining access to a major chain’s distribution, reservation system, marketing, and loyalty program.[1][2][3]\n\nThe main types worth considering are:\n\n- **Corporate soft brands**: these are affiliated with major hotel groups and are designed for independent or historic hotels that want brand support without a heavy, standardized identity.[1][2][4]\n- **Collection brands**: these are often the best fit when the owner wants even more freedom, since they tend to preserve more of the hotel’s unique style and operating flexibility while still providing system-wide sales and marketing support.[4][6]\n- **Voluntary or marketing consortia**: these can also preserve individuality, but they are usually less integrated than a corporate soft brand and may offer weaker system benefits.[4][6]\n\nExamples of brand families an owner would typically review include:\n\n- **Marriott**: Autograph Collection, The Luxury Collection\n- **Hilton**: Curio Collection, Tapestry Collection\n- **Hyatt**: The Unbound Collection, JdV by Hyatt\n- **IHG**: Vignette Collection\n- **Accor**: MGallery, Handwritten Collection\n- **Preferred Hotels & Resorts**\n- **Small Luxury Hotels of the World (SLH)**[2][3][6]\n\nIf the priority is *maximum individuality*, the best fit is usually a **collection brand** or a light-touch **soft brand** rather than a traditional hard brand, because these models are specifically built to combine identity preservation with chain-level reach.[2][4][6]",
